Update: I can replicate the bug with the following minimal example:
```c
// main.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>

extern unsigned char  _binary__bin_busybox_start;
extern unsigned char  _binary__bin_busybox_end;

int main(int argc, char *const argv[])
{
  void *start = (void *) &_binary__bin_busybox_start;
  size_t size = (size_t)(&_binary__bin_busybox_end - &_binary__bin_busybox_start);
  FILE *file = fopen("/tmp/busybox", "wb");
  int fd = fileno(file);
  write(fd, start, size);
  fchmod(fd, S_IRUSR | S_IWUSR | S_IXUSR);
  fclose(file);
  return 0;
}
```
And compiling in the following way:
```
objcopy --input-target binary --output-target elf64-x86-64 --binary-architecture i386:x86-64 /bin/busybox busybox.o
gcc -o app main.c busybox.o
```

In this way it works with both glibc and musl, but when I compile with the `-O2` flag I receive a `SIGILL` on the `write` call when running `./app`. This bug only happens in the optimized builds with musl, while on glibc it still works perfectly.

Alpine uses "standalone `fortify-headers`" which emit `SIGILL`, and is disabled with -O0 but enabled with -O2.

In their private copy of [`write`](https://git.2f30.org/fortify-headers/file/include/unistd.h.html#l146):

```c
_FORTIFY_FN(write) ssize_t write(int __f, const void *__s, size_t __n)
{
	size_t __b = __builtin_object_size(__s, 0);
 
	if (__n > __b)
		__builtin_trap();
	return __orig_write(__f, __s, __n);
}
```

Since the compiler does not know how big `start` is, this comparison fails and you crash.  This means the fault is not in musl nor your code, but specifically Alpine.  Try on Void, Adelie, or Sabotage.

It does know how big start is: it's 1 byte, because that's how you declared it. Instead of:

```
extern unsigned char  _binary__bin_busybox_start;
extern unsigned char  _binary__bin_busybox_end;
```

try:

```
extern unsigned char  _binary__bin_busybox_start[];
extern unsigned char  _binary__bin_busybox_end[];
```

then remove the `&` (address-of) operators in the code below.

